Name     pwr12;
PartNo   00;
Date     2026-01-01;
Revision 01;
Designer decpld;
Company  decpld;
Assembly none;
Location none;
Device   g22v10;

/* EXPECT refusal

   Pin 12 is GND (atf22v10c-datasheet Table 2-1, Figure 2-2). This
   asks the oracle to use it as a signal anyway.

   The expected result is a REFUSAL, not a JEDEC file: WinCUPL reports
   "invalid input" in the .lst and produces no output. That refusal is
   the measurement. A pinout diagram says which pins are supply rails;
   this says the compiler agrees, which is what makes "not usable by a
   design" a checked claim rather than a transcription of a diagram.

   run.sh reads the "EXPECT refusal" line above and reports the refusal
   as a result, exiting 0, so a batch run of the suite does not stop
   here. It also fails loudly if the oracle ever ACCEPTS this design,
   because that would mean the claim is wrong.

   The marker sits inside a CUPL comment so it cannot be mistaken for a
   declaration. That it is inert is checked rather than assumed: see
   marker-inert.pld, which carries the same line and must compile. */

PIN 12 = a;
PIN 23 = o0;

o0 = a;
